WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.136
Before anything else, picture this.

00:00:02.536 --> 00:00:10.721
For every day of temperatures, find how long until a warmer day. Checking every pair is slow. A monotonic stack does it in one pass.

00:00:11.921 --> 00:00:12.850
Pause and think.

00:00:13.250 --> 00:00:17.581
When a new taller item arrives, what happens to shorter items on the stack?

00:00:17.981 --> 00:00:25.295
Hold your answer; we will check it at the end. And whenever a new word comes up, we stop for a moment and explain it.

00:00:26.495 --> 00:00:33.066
A monotonic stack finds, for every item, the next item that is bigger or smaller, in one pass.

00:00:33.078 --> 00:00:42.145
New word: stack. Last-in-first-out structure; also the memory area holding function calls. Picture it like this: A stack of plates.

00:00:42.545 --> 00:00:54.991
New word: monotonic stack. A stack kept always increasing (or decreasing) to find next-greater elements fast. Picture it like this: A line of people where shorter people step out whenever a taller one arrives.

00:00:57.528 --> 00:01:01.046
Picture people standing in a line at a parade, looking right.

00:01:01.496 --> 00:01:05.710
Short people behind a tall person can never be seen past that tall person.

00:01:06.160 --> 00:01:11.617
So when a tall person arrives, every shorter person waiting has found their answer, and they leave.

00:01:12.067 --> 00:01:16.676
The waiting people always stand from tallest to shortest. That is the monotonic stack.

00:01:18.026 --> 00:01:21.347
Daily temperatures: how many days until a warmer day?

00:01:21.797 --> 00:01:26.859
We push day indexes. The stack keeps temperatures decreasing from bottom to top.

00:01:27.309 --> 00:01:33.613
When seventy five arrives, it is warmer than seventy one and seventy three, so both pop and get their answers.

00:01:34.063 --> 00:01:38.080
Each day is pushed once and popped once, so the whole thing is linear.

00:01:39.430 --> 00:01:41.044
How to choose the direction.

00:01:41.494 --> 00:01:46.707
Want next greater? Keep the stack decreasing, and pop while the new item is bigger.

00:01:47.157 --> 00:01:51.789
Want next smaller? Keep it increasing, and pop while the new item is smaller.

00:01:52.239 --> 00:01:56.883
Store indexes, not values, so you can compute distances and widths.

00:01:58.233 --> 00:02:00.230
Where it shows up in interviews.

00:02:00.680 --> 00:02:04.860
Largest rectangle in a histogram and maximal rectangle in a matrix.

00:02:05.310 --> 00:02:09.605
Trapping rain water, where a pop finds a basin between two walls.

00:02:10.055 --> 00:02:13.561
Stock span and removing digits to make the smallest number.

00:02:14.911 --> 00:02:20.705
Keep a stack sorted, pop when the new item breaks the order, and the popped item has found its answer.

00:02:20.676 --> 00:02:21.930
Back to the question.

00:02:22.330 --> 00:02:26.660
When a new taller item arrives, what happens to shorter items on the stack?

00:02:27.060 --> 00:02:29.812
They are popped, and the new item is their answer.

00:02:31.012 --> 00:02:31.883
Quick recap.

00:02:32.283 --> 00:02:33.513
Keep the stack sorted.

00:02:33.913 --> 00:02:35.283
Pop when the rule breaks.

00:02:35.683 --> 00:02:37.820
Each item pushed and popped once.
